  "criteria": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "level": "Adequate",
      "mark": 6,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "where": "Introduction",
          "quote": "Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."
        },
        {
          "where": "Introduction",
          "quote": "allow marine and conservation scientists to more accurately predict and react to coral bleaching events."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The mission rationale and payload concept (fluid lensing for coral bleaching) are well explained and clearly benefit Australia. The single-satellite architecture is justified based on the timescale of bleaching events. However, users are only loosely identified as 'marine and conservation scientists' without distinguishing formal operating bodies from commercial or governmental customer entities. Referencing in the introduction is also quite sparse."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "level": "Adequate",
      "mark": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "where": "Mission Design",
          "quote": "with a latitude of 45.05°N."
        },
        {
          "where": "Orbit",
          "quote": "requires a mean inclination of 87.7924° for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."
        },
        {
          "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
          "quote": "An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "Major design aspects are covered using STK access analyses and trade-offs. However, several substantial technical errors occur: Kourou's latitude is incorrectly stated as 45.05°N rather than ~5.2°N; the 600 km sun-synchronous orbit inclination is reported as prograde 87.79° instead of retrograde 97.79° (contradicting the student's own MATLAB plot); and an average contact duration of 580.8 seconds (~9.7 minutes) is incorrectly described as 'almost twenty minutes'."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "level": "Adequate",
      "mark": 6,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "where": "Conclusion",
          "quote": "The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The conclusion recaps the core mission elements, connecting the launch vehicle, sun-synchronous orbit, and Tasmanian ground station back to the coral monitoring objective. However, it remains high-level and descriptive, lacking critical reflection on design limitations, operational risks, or specific technical recommendations."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "level": "Adequate",
      "mark": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "where": "Mission Design",
          "quote": "a wiki sorted directory – ‘Timeline of spaceflight’ – will be referred to throughout the report [6]."
        },
        {
          "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
          "quote": "utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes [10]."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "Structure and readability are acceptable, but professional presentation is lacking. Wikipedia is explicitly cited despite task instructions forbidding it; raw launch tables are pasted directly with orphan superscript tags; figures lack formal numbering and captions; reference [10] is corrupted; the bibliography consists only of bare URLs; and an incomplete sentence is left in the EOL section."
    }
